beads.body:
Add legalityCheck() to plant/production.mjs: today a structurally illegal network (fan-out, a cycle, an edge with no shared resource or an ambiguous one, an unknown node kind, a duplicate id, a non-positive rate/demand/capacity, a processor with zero inputs or outputs) is only reported as a THROWN exception from feasible() — every one of those cases is already pinned in plant/test/production.selftest.mjs's throws() checks, but nothing in this tree can show a visitor WHICH reason fired without wrapping feasible() in a try/catch and parsing a developer-console error message. LEVEL_1 and this turn's other proposal (LEVEL_2) only ever exercise the QUANTITATIVE refusal path (feasible-but-short); the STRUCTURAL refusal path (illegal wiring) has zero player-legible surface. This is deliberately backend-only prerequisite work for a future 'wire it wrong' level, and it does not touch index.html or level-view.js, so it cannot collide with the other proposal in this turn.

Bounded paths: plant/production.mjs (add one new export, do not touch feasible/band/analyse's existing behaviour), plant/test/legality.selftest.mjs (new, createsGate).

plant/production.mjs already defines (but does not export) analyse({ nodes, edges }), which throws Error with a distinct message per cause. Add a second, non-throwing entry point around that SAME internal function, additive only:
  export function legalityCheck(network) {
    try { analyse(network); return { legal: true, reason: null }; }
    catch (e) { return { legal: false, reason: e.message }; }
  }
Do not change analyse's throw sites, do not change feasible()'s signature or its own throwing behaviour — plant/test/production.selftest.mjs's throws(() => feasible(...)) assertions are the existing pin for every one of these causes and must keep passing without that file being touched at all.

Gate (plant/test/legality.selftest.mjs, does not exist yet — create it): import legalityCheck and feasible from '../production.mjs'. Reuse the exact fixture objects already in plant/test/production.selftest.mjs's 'fan-out and cycles', 'resource matching' and 'other refusals' sections (copy them in, do not import test-from-test) and for each assert legalityCheck(fixture).legal === false and .reason is a string containing the keyword production.mjs's own Error message already uses for that cause (e.g. 'fan-out', 'cycle', 'no shared resource', 'ambiguous', 'unknown node kind', 'duplicate id'). Assert legalityCheck on a plain valid source->processor->sink fixture (reuse production.selftest.mjs's first case) and on plant/levels/level1.mjs's LEVEL_1 both return { legal: true, reason: null }. Finally, using the same throws() helper pattern as production.selftest.mjs, assert feasible() still throws on every one of those illegal fixtures — a direct regression check that this ticket did not change feasible()'s contract. Must also still pass unmodified: node plant/test/production.selftest.mjs.

BOUNDED PATHS (R3): plant/index.html only.

THE BUG. plant/level-view.js's drawLevel() (imported and called identically by all six levels) writes `class="ln"` on every node's id label and `class="ls ${short ? 'bad' : ''}"` on every node's second line — 'bad' is set exactly when that node is a starved sink (level-view.js:82-91). plant/index.html's <style> block declares the rules that give those classes their look:

  svg#lvl .ln { font: 600 14px ui-serif, Georgia, serif; fill: var(--fg); }
  svg#lvl .ls { font: 12px ui-monospace, Menlo, monospace; fill: var(--dim); }
  svg#lvl .ls.bad { fill: var(--bad); }

`svg#lvl` is an EXACT id selector — it matches only the Level 1 <svg id="lvl">. The other five levels' SVGs (id="lvl2" through id="lvl6", all present and confirmed in index.html) get none of these rules: their node-id text and status-line text render in whatever the browser's default text style is, and critically, a starved node's 'bad' class never turns red on Levels 2-6. The rect border DOES still turn red on those levels (its stroke colour is an inline `var(--bad)` attribute set directly by drawLevel(), not CSS-class-based), so the levels aren't silently broken — but the text half of the 'this failed' signal, on 5 of 6 levels, is missing. This is a correctness gap in an already-declared, already-shipped behaviour, not a new decoration: '.ls.bad' exists and is applied by the shared renderer every level uses; the CSS just never reaches it outside Level 1.

THE FIX. Give every level's <svg id="lvl..."> a shared class, e.g. `class="lvl-svg"` (six edits, one attribute each: <svg id="lvl" class="lvl-svg" ...>, <svg id="lvl2" class="lvl-svg" ...>, ... <svg id="lvl6" class="lvl-svg" ...>), and change the three CSS rules to target that shared class instead of the id:

  .lvl-svg .ln { font: 600 14px ui-serif, Georgia, serif; fill: var(--fg); }
  .lvl-svg .ls { font: 12px ui-monospace, Menlo, monospace; fill: var(--dim); }
  .lvl-svg .ls.bad { fill: var(--bad); }

(A shared class rather than six repeated id-selectors — same shape as the `.lvl-verdict`/`.lvl-btn` rules immediately below it in the same <style> block, which already work this way across all six levels.) The old `svg#lvl` id-scoped rules may be removed or left as a harmless duplicate; the gate below only requires the shared-class rule to exist and every level's svg to carry the class.

THE GATE — a new file, plant/test/index-level-css.selftest.mjs, following the exact text-based house style of plant/test/index-level6-wiring.selftest.mjs (fs.readFileSync on index.html, substring/regex assertions, no DOM/browser — this repo's established pattern for grading index.html since it has no build step). Assert:
  1. Each of the six level svg tags (id="lvl", id="lvl2", id="lvl3", id="lvl4", id="lvl5", id="lvl6") carries `class="lvl-svg"` in its opening tag.
  2. The <style> block contains a selector rule for `.lvl-svg .ln`, one for `.lvl-svg .ls`, and one for `.lvl-svg .ls.bad` (regex against the raw <style> text, same technique the existing wiring tests use against <script> text).
  3. CONTROL: assert the OLD bug reproduces against a literal string that only scopes `svg#lvl .ln` with no class-based rule anywhere — i.e. write the check so that reverting the CSS change (removing the .lvl-svg rules) makes this test fail, not just checking presence in isolation. Concretely: fail if `.lvl-svg .ln` is absent, regardless of whether `svg#lvl .ln` is present — that proves the check is pinned to the fix, not merely to any styling existing somewhere.
  4. Regression: Levels 1-6's existing wiring (LEVEL_1 through LEVEL_6 imports, per-level svg/verdict mount ids) is still present and unchanged, mirroring the 'levels 1-5's existing wiring is still intact' block every prior index-levelN-wiring test already carries (R6 — reversible, no other wiring touched).

R5 memory: this bug and its shared-class fix shape were already reasoned about in a prior planning turn's decision log ('CSS fix specified as a shared .lvl-svg class, not six repeated id-selectors') but never landed — this ticket is that fix, now with a concrete gate. Distinct from the earlier 'band-colour' ticket the ledger records as rejected-by-review with no reason given (ask lp-8f8836): that ticket proposed NEW colour meaning; this one restores an ALREADY-DECLARED colour rule to five levels where it silently never applied. R4: independent of every currently-open bead — touches only index.html's <style> block and its six <svg> tags.

`.github/loop/verdicts/<turn>.json` records `gate: "failed"` and nothing else. Every selftest in plant/ already prints `✗ <assertion name> — <detail>` for each failure and exits 1; loop-work runs the command, reads the exit code, and DISCARDS the stdout that explains it.

THE COST IS MEASURED, NOT HYPOTHETICAL. lp-250e23 failed its gate on turn 55 and was re-dispatched to turn 56. Turn 56's brief carried the whole ledger and could not carry the one fact it needed. It spent most of its budget reverse-engineering a runtime failure from source with no shell — verifying refusal field names, push order, the AXES wall table, floating-point exactness at the plan corners, and every regex in a sibling gate — and STILL did not identify the cause. One line of captured stdout would have replaced all of it.

This compounds with the already-recorded fact that a failed turn's outbox is never applied: the successor gets neither the predecessor's findings NOR the failure reason, and re-derives both. That is how a bead burns three turns on one defect.

SCOPE: in `.github/workflows/loop-work.yml`, tee the gate command's combined output to a file, keep a bounded tail of it (the failing lines are at the end; 4000 chars is ample and matches the outbox body cap's order of magnitude), and write it into the verdict JSON as `gateOutput`. Then include it in the brief for a turn dispatched to a bead whose previous verdict failed — that second half is the point, and without it the data is captured and still unread.

BOUNDED PATHS: `.github/workflows/loop-work.yml`, and whichever of `scripts/loop-*.mjs` composes the verdict and the brief.

WHAT THE GATE MUST CHECK — and this needs a NEW test file, because nothing currently tests the verdict writer:
· a verdict written for a FAILING command carries `gateOutput` containing that command's stderr AND stdout, not just one;
· the tail is bounded, asserted by feeding a command that emits far more than the cap and checking the recorded length;
· the TAIL is kept rather than the head — assert that a marker printed as the LAST line survives and one printed first does not, because the failing assertions are at the end and a head-clip would keep only the banner;
· a PASSING command records no `gateOutput` (or an empty one), so a green verdict does not carry a kilobyte of noise;
· a brief composed for a bead whose last verdict failed CONTAINS that output, and one for a fresh bead does not. This is the assertion that makes the change worth anything.

WHY class-a + createsGate: no test exists for the verdict writer, so the check must be built. NOTE THE CONTAINMENT PROBLEM HONESTLY: the bounded paths are outside the fleet's write scope (`.github/loop/`, `plant/`), so this is NOT fleet-dispatchable as written and a seat would fail it exactly the way lp-14c7f5 failed five times. It needs a human, or it needs the write scope widened first. Filing it anyway because the diagnosis is worth having recorded; whoever promotes it must resolve that first.

summon-session.selftest.mjs carried this, verbatim, at the foot of its 'caller' block:

    // Only the kernel's answer is asserted — pinning `placement.mjs`'s
    // behaviour here would turn a future fix of it into a red gate. The
    // predicate's answer is logged instead.
    console.log(`  · note: legalSeed([NaN,...]).ok === ${legalSeed(...).ok} — every
      comparison against NaN is false, so the predicate has no hull violation...`)

That reasoning is CORRECT and the ledger already praises it: a turn that records a bug it is not allowed to fix should log the buggy value and assert only the stable one, so the fix does not arrive as a red gate. It worked exactly as designed — placement.mjs was fixed later and no gate went red.

The cost, which nobody costed at the time: the log now prints `legalSeed([NaN,...]).ok === false` immediately followed by a sentence explaining why it is true. A stale ASSERTION goes red and gets fixed within the hour. A stale LOG is only noticed by a human reading test output, and nothing in this loop reads test output — the harness records pass/fail. So the deferred-assertion trick converts a loud future failure into a silent permanent one, and the silence is unbounded in time.

Not an argument against the trick; the alternative was worse. It is an argument for the missing half of it: WHEN YOU LOG INSTEAD OF ASSERTING BECAUSE THE BEHAVIOUR IS WRONG, THE LOG IS A DEBT AND SOMETHING HAS TO COLLECT IT. Two cheap collectors, either sufficient. (1) File the follow-up bead in the same turn — 'when placement.mjs refuses NaN, turn this log into an assertion' — so the debt is in the queue rather than in a comment. (2) Make the log SELF-INVALIDATING: assert the disagreement rather than printing it, i.e. `ok(legalSeed(...).ok === true, 'KNOWN BUG: remove this when placement.mjs is fixed')`. That goes red on the FIX, which reads alarming, but it is red for exactly one turn and the message tells that turn what to do. Option 2 is strictly more reliable and it is the one I would take next time; option 1 is politer to whoever is on call.

I collected this one by hand: the log is now an assertion that the predicate refuses and names the non-finite axis, so the three layers (predicate, kernel, session) are pinned as agreeing rather than one of them being narrated.

The ticket frames this as two verbs disagreeing. Reading the stack, it is one verb disagreeing with everything below it.

  placement.mjs   legalSummon()      returns { ok, solid, centre, refusals, first }
  foamworld.js    reformPocketAll()  returns { ok, pocket, planted, refusals, first }
  summon-session  preview()          returns { ..., refusals, first }
  summon-session  place()            returned { ..., refusals, REFUSAL }

foamworld.js line 912 is the clearest: `const refuse = (refusals) => ({ ok:false, pocket:null, planted:[], refusals, first: refusals[0] })`. So the transaction place() wraps ALREADY calls it `first`, and place() renamed it on the way out.

That settles a question the ticket left open and I would otherwise have had to guess at: if the two names are ever collapsed to one, the survivor is `first`, because three of the four producers already say it and only one consumer (summon-view.js) reads the other. It also explains why the asymmetry felt like a slip rather than a design — it was one.

The collapse is still NOT this ticket's work and I did not do it; a rename that misses a caller is strictly worse than an alias. But the direction is now recorded rather than a coin toss for whoever takes it.

THE DEFECT. `preview()`'s refusals carry `summonSeed` (from `legalSummon`); `place()`'s carry `point` (from `reformPocketAll`'s batch index). They mean the identical thing — the index within `con.seeds`, 0 being the centre — because `place()` hands `con.seeds` to the kernel in order and the kernel indexes the array it was given. Self-collisions likewise disagree: `otherSummonSeed` vs `otherPoint`.

WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN THE `first`/`refusal` split just closed. That one produced a visible `res.refusal || res.first` the first time anyone wrote a shared renderer. This one produces NOTHING visible: `highlight(rf.summonSeed)` renders correctly for every previewed refusal and highlights `undefined` — i.e. nothing, with no error — for exactly the summons the player actually attempted. `placement.mjs`'s own docstring already promises 'Every refusal carries `summonSeed`', and for the place path that promise is not kept.

SCOPE, deliberately the same conservative shape as lp-aabb74: in `SummonSession._attribute`, when a refusal carries a numeric `point` and no `summonSeed`, set `summonSeed` from it (and `otherSummonSeed` from `otherPoint` on a `batch` refusal). ADD, never rename: `point` and `otherPoint` keep their values and meanings, so nothing that reads them breaks and no caller has to be found. Removing either name is explicitly NOT in scope.

DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED: `closure` and `nav` refusals carry `points` (plural, the whole batch) and no index, because a closure failure genuinely cannot be attributed to one seed. They must be left alone — inventing a `summonSeed` for them would be a lie, and the gate should assert they still have none.

WHAT THE GATE MUST CHECK, in `plant/test/summon-session.selftest.mjs` §10, which already reaches every fixture needed:
  · for BOTH verbs, every `hull` / `seed` / `self` / `batch` refusal carries a numeric `summonSeed` in `[0, con.seeds.length)`;
  · the place-path `summonSeed` EQUALS its own `point` — so the normalisation is a copy and not a recomputation that could drift;
  · for the fixture in §4 (two cubes 5m apart, exactly one colliding pair, already proven to blame B's -x neighbour at batch index 2) preview and place name THE SAME index for the same geometry. That cross-verb agreement is the assertion that makes this worth doing, and it is the one a per-verb field-list check cannot make;
  · a `batch` refusal carries `otherSummonSeed === otherPoint`;
  · §10's existing 'place names point and NOT summonSeed' assertions are INVERTED by this work and must be rewritten, not deleted — they become 'names both, and they agree'.

WHY class-a: `plant/test/summon-session.selftest.mjs` exists and passes, and every fixture this needs is already in it. BOUNDED PATHS: plant/summon-session.mjs, plant/test/summon-session.selftest.mjs.

[turn 69 — done] THIRD TURN ON THIS BEAD, AND I FOUND WHY THE FIRST TWO FAILED. Turn 2's outbox (itself carrying turn 1's) was still on disk unapplied when I opened it; everything in it is carried forward below, marked CARRIED, because writing this file naively destroys it. The implementation in `summon-session.mjs` was already correct and I did not touch it. THE GATE WAS GENUINELY FAILING, on one hand-derived literal in the hull block of §10: `ok(snap(hullOf(hpv)) === snap([0, 2]) && ...)`. The fixture is a cube at r=1.6 centred on [0.5, 18.9, 38], and the expected hull-blamed set is NOT [0, 2] — it is [0, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]. A cube's neighbours sit at exactly ±2r along ONE axis each, so only indices 1 and 2 change x at all; the four y/z neighbours (3, 4, 5, 6) INHERIT the centre's x = 0.5 and are therefore outside the x >= 1 wall exactly as the centre is. Six of the seven seeds are out of hull, and only index 1 (+x, at x = 3.7) is inside. Verified from the sources rather than from the prior turns' prose: `hullBounds` is x in [1, W-1] (placement.mjs:79-85) and `clampSeed` the same (foamworld.js:761-768) with W = 80; `solids.mjs`:67 orders cube normals +x, -x, +y, -y, +z, -z; both `legalSeed` (hull wins per seed, placement.mjs:206-217) and `reformPocketAll` (hull then `continue`, foamworld.js:925-935) therefore emit exactly six hull refusals and agree on which six. FIXED, and deliberately fixed with more than the corrected literal: the expected set is now RECOMPUTED in the test from `pocket.W/H/D` against the constellation the fixture actually built — not via `hullViolation` or `legalSeed`, so it is an independent route — with the corrected literal kept ALONGSIDE it, because the recomputation catches a wrong expectation and the literal catches a moved fixture and neither alone catches both. Added the control the section was missing: index 1 is inside the wall and neither verb blames it, so the blamed set is a PROPER SUBSET rather than 'the whole shape' (which is the lazy implementation that would have passed the old assertion and the new one without it). Also made both set-comparison sorts NUMERIC — the default is lexicographic, which agrees with numeric order for a 7-seed cube and silently disagrees at 13 (dodecahedron) or 21 (icosahedron) seeds, so those helpers were a flake waiting for anyone who reused them on a bigger solid. HONESTLY: I had no Bash and did not run the gate. I re-derived the rest of §10 independently (the FIX.c2 cross-verb [2], the ten tiny-cube pairs, the closure/nav exemption, the direct-kernel control) and found no second defect, but that is inference. I also cannot prove this literal is the ONLY reason the earlier turns failed, only that it is a certain failure that was present in both.

WHY NOW. `summonSeed` is the index within `con.seeds` of the part of the summon that was refused, and as of lp-fcf387 BOTH verbs carry it — preview and place, hull/seed/self/batch alike. `placement.mjs`'s docstring has promised since it shipped that the field exists 'so a caller can light up the offending part of the shape', and no caller ever has. The panel currently renders one sentence and draws the whole constellation the same colour whether it landed or was refused.

THE WORK, and it is small because the plan renderer already exists: `summon-view.js`'s `planShapes(pocket, bounds, cands, cursor, originCount)` returns plain descriptors that `index.html` turns into SVG. Add the refused summon's seeds as descriptors carrying a `blamed: true` flag for the indices named by the refusals (`summonSeed`, plus `otherSummonSeed` on a self/batch refusal), so the page can draw the offending seed differently from its siblings. The page must stay decision-free — it reads a flag, it does not compute legality — which is what `index-summon-wiring.selftest.mjs` already asserts and must keep asserting.

WHY class-a: `plant/test/summon-view.selftest.mjs` exists and passes, and it already drives a REAL session on the MACRO fixture and reaches hull, pocket, player and self blames. The new assertions go in that file.

WHAT THE GATE MUST CHECK, and the weak version is not enough:
  - for a refused preview AND a refused place of the same geometry, the descriptor set marks exactly the indices the verdict's refusals name — recomputed in the test from `res.refusals`, not read back from the descriptor;
  - a CONTROL that the marked set is a PROPER SUBSET of the summon's seeds. Marking everything passes any 'the blamed seed is marked' check and is exactly the lazy implementation to guard against;
  - a self/batch refusal marks BOTH ends (`summonSeed` and `otherSummonSeed`);
  - a `closure`/`nav` refusal marks NOTHING, because it carries no index — the exemption lp-fcf387 pinned, now enforced at the render layer where a lie would be visible;
  - a SUCCESS marks nothing.

WHY A WEAKER CHECK WOULD NOT DO: asserting 'some seed is marked' passes for an implementation that marks the centre unconditionally, which is the single most likely wrong answer here (the centre is index 0 and it is what a naive loop reaches first). The subset control plus the recomputed-from-refusals comparison are what make it bite.

BOUNDED PATHS: plant/summon-view.js, plant/test/summon-view.selftest.mjs, plant/index.html, plant/test/index-summon-wiring.selftest.mjs.

[CARRIED BY TURNS 2 AND 3: this is vision item 1 work — it changes what a visitor sees — and it has now been proposed three times without reaching the ledger. TURN 3 ADDS ONE THING THE PROPER-SUBSET CONTROL MUST GET RIGHT, learned the hard way this turn: on an out-of-hull fixture SIX OF A CUBE'S SEVEN SEEDS are legitimately blamed, because the four y/z neighbours inherit the centre's out-of-bounds x. So the subset control must pick a fixture where the expected marked set is genuinely small, or recompute it rather than assume 'one or two seeds'.]

[turn 83 — done] THE DELIVERABLE WAS ALREADY ON DISK WHEN THIS TURN STARTED, complete and coherent across all four bounded paths — plant/summon-view.js exports blamedSeeds() and planShapes() takes a `res` argument and emits `blamed` on every summon descriptor; plant/index.html imports planShapes, passes lastRes, and reads s.blamed to pick stroke and width without computing legality; plant/test/index-summon-wiring.selftest.mjs asserts the export exists, that planShapes uses it rather than a second copy, that the page passes the verdict and reads the flag, and that index 0 survives the guard; plant/test/summon-view.selftest.mjs carries §11 (blamedSeeds pure) and §11b (a real pristine session, both verbs). I did NOT rebuild any of it. What I did instead was verify it by hand — no Bash this turn — and then close the one discriminating gap I found. VERIFIED BY HAND, in depth: (1) summonAt() takes aniso from pocket.opts.aniso exactly as place() does, so preview and place build byte-identical constellations and the cross-verb set equality in §11b cannot fail for a constellation-mismatch reason; (2) the §11b `fix` search is sound — p = s - off[1] puts summon seed 1 exactly on a pocket seed (gap 0) while the BAND filter forces gaps[0] >= 2.5, so the centre is provably unblamable by seed and, at 0.5 m inside hullBounds, unblamable by hull either, which is what makes the `!mk.has(0)` control real rather than lucky; (3) the tiny-cube self block at r=0.4 — I recomputed all 21 pairs: centre-to-x/z 0.8, centre-to-y 1.1866, x-to-z 1.131, x-to-y 1.4314, +x-to--x 1.6, +y-to--y 2.373. Ten pairs refuse under preview (sqrt vs 1.5) and the same ten under the kernel (square vs 2.25), with the closest to the boundary 0.069 away, so the preview/place set equality there is safe from the sqrt-vs-square divergence rather than lucky; (4) `landed` at line 246 is a top-level `let`, so the closing block at 735 is in scope. THE GAP I FOUND AND CLOSED: every assertion in §11's pure block had exactly ONE refusal in its fixture, so every one of them is satisfied by an implementation that reads res.refusals[0] (or res.first, the same object) and never walks the list — which is precisely the under-reporting shape this ledger has recorded four times. The fixture blocks do catch it, but only as a side effect of the tiny cube happening to foul itself in ten places. Added one assertion with three refusals (an indexed one, an indexless closure in the middle, and a pair) plus a control that the indexless middle element contributed nothing; the real blamedSeeds returns {1,4,6} and a head-only implementation returns {1}. HONESTLY STATED: I could not run `node plant/test/summon-view.selftest.mjs` — no Bash — so the two new assertions are hand-traced against the source of blamedSeeds, not executed, and my verification of the pre-existing sections is inference from reading rather than a green run.

WHY. lp-53afb4 corrected `legalSummon`'s docstring: the `metric` refusal carries neither `summonSeed` nor `at`, so a renderer written from the old sentence (`refusals.map((r) => highlight(r.summonSeed))`) lights up `undefined`, silently, with all the words around it intact. The predicate side is now pinned. The RENDERER side is not, and the renderer is the consumer the wrong sentence was aimed at.

`plant/summon-view.js`'s `summonSentence` branches on `blame`, and its gate already re-extracts each printed number with a clause-specific regex and compares it to the field it came from — a strong discipline. What it cannot currently reach is a `metric` verdict, because `summonAt` takes `aniso` from the pocket and the session therefore cannot produce one.

WHAT TO BUILD, in `plant/test/summon-view.selftest.mjs`:

  · hand a `metric` verdict to the renderer directly — build a constellation with a deliberately wrong `aniso` and call `legalSummon` on the real MACRO pocket, exactly as `placement.selftest.mjs` section 6 does, then wrap it in the shape the renderer consumes. Say in a comment that this route is synthetic BECAUSE the session cannot produce one, so a later reader does not 'simplify' it into a session call and silently delete the coverage;
  · assert the sentence is non-empty and that `!/undefined|NaN|\[object Object\]/.test(line)` — the cheap check that catches a template literal interpolating a missing field while every substring assertion around it still passes;
  · assert the sentence names both metrics, extracted with a clause-specific regex and compared to `conAniso` and `pocketAniso` off the refusal, not to a literal — the ledger records that comparing a printed value to a literal you chose measures your literal;
  · a CONTROL that the same check is not vacuous: a renderer that ignores the refusal entirely and prints a fixed apology would pass 'non-empty and no undefined', so at least one assertion must fail for a sentence that does not carry the two numbers.

WHAT WOULD MAKE THIS UNNECESSARY: if the renderer is later given a verdict shape that cannot express `metric` at all. It is not today — `preview()` returns `legalSummon`'s refusals verbatim.

WHY class-a: `plant/test/summon-view.selftest.mjs` exists and passes today. BOUNDED PATHS: plant/summon-view.js, plant/test/summon-view.selftest.mjs.

WHY. §11 of `summon-session.selftest.mjs` now pins that `role` ('centre' / 'neighbour') is on PREVIEW `hull` and `seed` refusals and on NO place refusal. That is the same asymmetry the `summonSeed`/`point` divergence was, one field over, and it fails the same way: a renderer saying 'the centre of the cube is in rock' works perfectly on every highlight and renders the string 'undefined' for exactly the summons the player actually attempted — silently, with all the words around it intact.

IT IS DERIVABLE, and by the identical argument that licensed the `summonSeed` copy: `place()` hands `reformPocketAll` exactly `con.seeds` in order, so index 0 IS the constellation's centre by construction. `_attribute` already knows the index (it has just copied it from `point`), so `role` is `summonSeed === 0 ? 'centre' : 'neighbour'`.

WHAT TO BUILD, in `plant/summon-session.mjs`:

  · in `_attribute`, set `role` on `hull` and `seed` refusals when `summonSeed` is a number and `role` is not already set — ADD, NEVER OVERWRITE, exactly as the `summonSeed` copy does, so the preview path's authoritative value is untouched;
  · NOT on `batch`/`self` — a pair has no single role, `placement.mjs` says so explicitly and lp-53afb4 recorded why. This is the assertion most likely to be got wrong by a turn that treats `role` as 'a field per-seed refusals have';
  · NOT on `closure`/`nav`/`empty`/`point`, which carry no index at all.

WHAT THE GATE MUST CHECK (`plant/test/summon-session.selftest.mjs` §11, which exists and passes today):

  · the two place literals `at,axis,blame,clamped,depth,limit,point,reason,summonSeed,value,wall` and `at,blame,gap,need,point,reason,seed,seedIndex,summonSeed` GAIN `role` and nothing else — the exact-set form is what makes that a real check rather than a presence one;
  · the `batch` literal is UNCHANGED, and that is the load-bearing negative;
