WHY. The exact-key-set discipline has now been applied twice and found something both times: `placement.selftest.mjs` §8 (lp-53afb4) turned up that `metric` carries no `summonSeed` and no `at` and that `self` carries no `role`; `summon-session.selftest.mjs` §11 turned up that `ok` is preview-only and `role` is preview-only. Two applications, four undocumented irregularities, every one of them invisible to a presence check.

`level.mjs`'s `placementReport` and `pocketLevel.mjs`'s `pocketPlacementReport` are the next two producers and nothing pins either. They are the ones a level renderer reads, and they have the most vocabularies to reconcile: `pocketPlacementReport` merges `placement.mjs`'s pocket reasons (`hull`, `seed`, `metric`) with the session-local ones (`self-collision`, `collides with existing summon`), and applies `buildcert.mjs`'s `BLAME_PRECEDENCE` on top. That is three sources of fields converging on one record, which is one more than `summon-session.mjs` had.

The ledger already records that these two are 'two accumulating walks over the same state view — close enough to look like duplication, and they are not'. An exact key set per reason per function is the cheapest possible statement of how they really differ, and it is the artefact that would make a future merge decision reviewable instead of a judgement call.

WHAT TO BUILD: a section in `plant/test/pocket-level.selftest.mjs` asserting `Object.keys(r).sort().join(',')` against a literal for every reason each function can produce, following §11's shape exactly:

